      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the possible outcomes for each die.\n2. Determine the favorable outcomes for the events described.\n3. Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling two six-sided dice.\n4. Calculate the probability of the desired event.\n\nFirst, let's identify the possible outcomes for each die:\n- A six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.\n\nNext, let's determine the favorable outcomes:\n- For the first die, rolling a number less than three is the same as rolling a 1 or a 2. So, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n- For the second die, rolling a number greater than three is the same as rolling a 4, 5, or 6. So, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n\nSince the rolls of the two dice are independent events, we can find the total number of favorable outcomes by multiplying the number of favorable outcomes for each die:\n\\[ \\text{Total favorable outcomes} = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\]\n\nThe probability of a specific combination of outcomes (either rolling a 1 or a 2 on the first die and rolling a 4, 5, or 6 on the second die) is:\n\\[ \\text{Probability of one specific outcome} = \\frac{2 \\times 3}{6 \\times 6} = \\frac{6}{36} = \\frac{1}{6} \\]\n\nSince there are 6 favorable outcomes out of a total of \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible outcomes, the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[ \\text{Total probability} = \\frac{6}{36} = \\frac{1}{6} \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}} \\]",
