      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die when rolling two six-sided dice.\n\nFirst, let's identify the possible outcomes for each die:\n- A six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.\n- The numbers less than three on the first die are 1 and 2.\n- The numbers greater than three on the second die are 4, 5, and 6.\n\nNext, we calculate the probability of each event:\n1. The probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability}( \\text{number less than 3} ) = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. The probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability}( \\text{number greater than 3} ) = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\nSince the rolls of the two dice are independent events, we multiply the probabilities of these individual events to find the combined probability:\n\\[\n\\text{Combined probability} = \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
